      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Summary\n\n| Quantity | Formula | What it measures |\n|---|---|---|\n| $a \\cdot b$ | $\\sum a_i b_i = \\lvert a\\rvert\\lvert b\\rvert\\cos\\theta$ | length and angle |\n| $\\operatorname{proj}_a b$ | $\\dfrac{a\\cdot b}{a \\cdot a}\\,a$ | the component along $a$ |\n| $a \\times b$ | $(a_2b_3-a_3b_2,\\;\\ldots)$ | area and orientation |\n| $[a,b,c]$ | $a \\cdot (b \\times c) = \\det(a;b;c)$ | signed volume |\n| $\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\ell)$ | $\\lvert w \\times d\\rvert / \\lvert d\\rvert$ | drop the parallel part |\n| $\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\Pi)$ | $\\lvert n\\cdot(x-p_0)\\rvert / \\lvert n\\rvert$ | keep the normal part |\n| $\\operatorname{dist}(\\ell_1,\\ell_2)$ | $\\lvert[p_2-p_1,d_1,d_2]\\rvert / \\lvert d_1\\times d_2\\rvert$ | volume ÷ base area |\n\nEvery distance formula here came from the same move: **split a vector into a component along something and a component perpendicular to it, then keep whichever one the problem cares about.** That is orthogonal decomposition, and it will not stop being the answer — it returns as Gram–Schmidt in linear algebra, as the normal component defining the second fundamental form in Stage 3.04, and as the splitting of a connection into horizontal and vertical parts in Stage 5.05.\n\n## Two things to carry forward\n\n**The determinant is a volume.** We saw it twice: the Cauchy–Schwarz gap factored into a squared $2\\times 2$ determinant, and the triple product *was* a $3\\times 3$ determinant. When Stage 3 computes surface area with $\\sqrt{EG-F^2}$ and Stage 4 writes the Riemannian volume element as $\\sqrt{\\det g}$, they are using this same fact.\n\n**The cross product is a three-dimensional accident.** Orthogonality, area and orientation all generalise; packaging them into a *vector* does not. Stage 4 replaces $a \\times b$ with the wedge product $a \\wedge b$, and the familiar identities reappear in a form that works in every dimension.\n\n## Next\n\n**1.02 — Isometries of the Plane** takes the dot product as given and asks which maps preserve it. The answer is more rigid than one might expect: every distance-preserving map of the plane is a composition of at most three reflections.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## References\n\nThe material here is standard first-year content; these are the places it is done well.\n\n- ★ Lee, N.-H. (2020) *Geometry: from Isometries to Special Relativity*. Cham: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-030-42101-4. Chapter 1 develops the Euclidean plane from the inner product in the same spirit.\n- Coxeter, H.S.M. (1969) *Introduction to Geometry*. 2nd edn. New York: Wiley. Chapters 1–2 for the classical solid geometry.\n- Hilbert, D. and Cohn-Vossen, S. (1952) *Geometry and the Imagination*. New York: Chelsea. For the geometric intuition behind the algebra.\n\nOn the remark that the cross product is a three-dimensional accident, and the wedge product is the general construction, see Stage 4 and:\n\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2013) *Introduction to Smooth Manifolds*. 2nd edn. New York: Springer. Chapter 14 (alternating tensors).\n- ★ Stillwell, J. (2005) *The Four Pillars of Geometry*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/0-387-29052-4. Pillar two develops exactly this inner-product foundation.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}